    },
    {
      "id": "thm-transfinite-recursion",
      "concern": "The uniqueness remark says: 'A construction phrased as \"at stage a pick some element not yet used\" is a different theorem and does cost a choice principle.' This needs an 'in general' qualification: finite successive selections require no choice axiom, and a supplied well-order can determine unused elements canonically. The stated recursion theorem and its proof are unaffected.",
      "severity": "presentation"
    },
    {
      "id": "fs-transfinite-induction-needs-choice",
      "concern": "The 'useful test' says that when the answer is 'some element with the following property' and there is generally more than one candidate, 'a choice function is being used and must be paid for.' Multiple candidates and informal selection language alone do not establish a choice-axiom cost: finite selections or a supplied canonical selector are choice-free. Qualify this heuristic consistently with the item's distinction between supplied data and unavailable selectors.",
      "severity": "presentation"
    }
  ],
